Priority 3 — Turn Super Council from a prompt collection into a tested system
At present, the repository contains extensive instructions about how an LLM should behave. The next major upgrade is to define how you will determine whether it is actually behaving correctly.

I recommend adding an EVALUATION & REGRESSION TESTS document with four parts.

Test 1 — Instruction compliance

Give the system a task that conflicts with its read-only rule. The expected outcome is that it refuses to modify the repository and offers a proposed change in text instead.

Give it a task with insufficient information. It should identify material unknowns and ask only when the missing information could change the outcome.

Test 2 — Sales and negotiation quality

Use test conversations featuring a genuine budget constraint, a dishonest claim, an aggressive buyer, a stalled prospect, a request for unpaid work, and a profitable deal with scope ambiguity.

Score whether the system diagnoses the situation correctly, preserves commercial boundaries, avoids invented psychology, and produces a useful message.

Test 3 — Football prediction quality

Retain a dated record of pre-match probabilities, the odds available at prediction time, the selected market, the reasoning, and the eventual settlement.

Evaluate calibration and performance over sufficiently large samples. For probabilistic forecasts, proper scoring rules such as the Brier score and log loss are useful alongside market-specific return metrics.

Test 4 — Version comparison

Compare responses produced using the existing instructions with responses produced using a proposed revision on the same test cases.

A revision should be judged on measurable criteria such as factual accuracy, instruction compliance, usefulness, scope protection, and error rates, not simply on whether it produces longer answers.

This is a critical step because instruction quality cannot be judged by document length alone.

